TITLE:: Breaks — the breakbeat machine
summary:: A tutorial: one drum loop, locked to your tempo, from intact to dust
categories:: Live Coding>Synesthesia
related:: Classes/Break, Guides/Synesthesia, Reference/Synesthesia-Reference, Classes/GrainBuf

SECTION:: What this is

A breakbeat machine for live coding. You point it at a folder of drum-loop
recordings — any tempos, all mixed together — and any one of them becomes a voice
that:

list::
## locks to your session tempo the moment it enters, whatever tempo it was recorded at,
## dissolves continuously from the intact loop, through beat-sized and
   sixteenth-sized chops, down to granular dust, on emphasis::one:: control,
## reorders its own slices — in place, reversed, resequenced, euclidean-gated, or
   at random, with a fader between order and chance,
## swaps to a different recording mid-phrase with no gap and no re-typing.
::

It is two SynthDefs and a registry class. The registry
(link::Classes/Break::) is the part that knows things — which files exist, what
tempo each was recorded at, which slice plays when. The engine
(code::\break::) is pure DSP driven by controls. That split is not cosmetic: a
SynthDef in this system is forbidden from touching interpreter state, so
everything that requires knowing about your library has to live in the language.

SECTION:: Before you start

You need the normal Synesthesia performance environment (see
link::Guides/Synesthesia::): a booted server, a link::Classes/ProxySpace:: in
code::p:: with a tempo clock, and the code::\break:: and code::\brk1:: SynthDefs
loaded. If you run this repo's code::Setup.scd::, all of that is done for you,
including the scan of your breaks folder.

SUBSECTION:: A library you can try right now

If you have no drum loops yet, you can still follow this whole tutorial using the
sound that ships with SuperCollider. Evaluate this to build a two-bar loop from it
and register it by hand:

code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav";
    var buf = Buffer.read(s, path, action: { |b|
        // it is not really a drum loop, but it is 2 beats long at 120 bpm
        Break.capture(\demo, b, 2);
        ("registered \\demo: " ++ b.duration.round(0.01) ++ "s").postln;
        Break.list;
    });
});
)
::

Once you have real loops, put them in a folder whose name starts with
code::breaks:: inside code::Setup/samples/:: and they load at boot. Everything
below then works on code::\amen:: instead of code::\demo::.

SECTION:: Your first chopped break

code::
p.clock.tempo = 2;        // 120 bpm, in beats per second
~d.brk(\demo);            // put the break in a proxy
~d.play;                  // and hear it
::

That is the whole entry gesture. code::~d:: is now a break voice: it holds the
engine, its own slice-sequence buffer, and the loop's beat length. It is playing
sixteenth-note chops in source order, fitted to 120 bpm.

Everything from here is one word at a time, and every word leaves the voice
running — nothing below rebuilds the synth or restarts the loop.

SECTION:: grind — the one control that matters

code::grind:: is the macro. It moves the material continuously from the whole loop
to granular dust, and it has three exact stations:

table::
## code::0.0::   || one grain per loop   || the loop plays intact
## code::0.5::   || one grain per slice  || clean chops on the grid
## code::1.0::   || 64 grains per beat   || dust
::

code::
~d.grind(0);       // the loop, whole
~d.grind(0.5);     // sixteenth-sized pieces  (the default)
~d.grind(0.75);    // each piece subdivided further
~d.grind(1);       // grain-sized pieces
::

There is no mode switch anywhere in that sweep. "Slicing" and "granulating" are
the same mechanism at different grain sizes — a slice emphasis::is:: a big grain
quantised to the grid — which is why the gesture is continuous.

The 0.5 station is exact, not approximate: at that value the grain grid and the
slice grid run at the same rate from the same phase, so each grain begins exactly
where a slice begins.

warning::
strong::Moving grind on its own will not sound like much,:: and this is worth
understanding before you decide the knob is broken. Pieces played in their
emphasis::original order, at their original times:: reassemble into the original
loop — at any resolution. That transparency is a feature: it means the machine adds
nothing until you ask it to.

code::grind:: does not transform the audio. It sets the emphasis::resolution:: at
which everything else operates. It becomes audible the moment a second gesture has
something to chew on:

code::
~d.grind(1);       // still basically the loop...
~d.jit(0.6);       // ...and now it is dust
~d.jit(0); ~d.grind(0.5); ~d.seq("0.325476");   // resolution you can hear
~d.scr(0.5);       // chance, at whatever size grind is set to
~d.over(0.5);      // gaps, at whatever size grind is set to
::

So the useful mental model is: code::grind:: chooses emphasis::how big the pieces
are::; code::seq::, code::scr::, code::jit:: and code::over:: choose emphasis::what
happens to them.:: A scramble at code::grind(0.3):: is a beat-shuffled break; the
same scramble at code::grind(0.9):: is a texture. Same word, different world,
because the resolution changed.
::

SUBSECTION:: over — how much each grain overruns its slot

code::
~d.over(1);        // grains abut: the default, tightest chops
~d.over(0.6);      // grains shorter than their slot: gaps, an MPC gate feel
~d.over(2.5);      // grains overlap: smeared, washy
~d.over(4);        // pad territory — feed it to a reverb and forget it is a drum loop
::

The level stays put as you move this: overlap is gain-compensated, measured flat
across the whole range.

SUBSECTION:: win — the grain envelope

Every grain is shaped by an envelope, and the choice matters much more for drums
than for pads: a smooth window fades in the front of every grain, which is exactly
where a snare's transient lives.

code::
~d.win(\punch);    // default: fast attack, short tail — chops keep their crack
~d.win(\gate);     // near-rectangular, hardest edges
~d.win(\hann);     // smooth: the classic granular window, mushy on drums
~d.win(\swell);    // reversed — each grain swells in
::

SECTION:: Reordering the slices

The slice order lives in a small buffer on the server that the language rewrites
in place, so resequencing never rebuilds anything. Changes land on the next bar.

code::
~d.seq("0325");            // play slices 0, 3, 2, 5 — then repeat
~d.seq("0.32 0.15");       // "." is a rest; spaces are just for your eyes
~d.seq("0123456789ab");    // digits run out at 9, then letters carry on
~d.rev;                    // the loop backwards by slice
~d.ord;                    // back to source order
::

A sequence may be longer than one loop, and it keeps counting across loops rather
than restarting — which gives you polymeter for free:

code::
~d.steps(16);              // 16 slices per loop
~d.seq("0123456789abcdef012345");   // 22 steps against a 16-slice loop
::

SUBSECTION:: From order to chance, on a fader

code::scr:: is the probability, per slice, that the engine ignores your sequence
and picks a slice at random instead. It is a continuous fade between composed and
stochastic, not a switch:

code::
~d.scr(0);         // your sequence, exactly
~d.scr(0.25);      // mostly your sequence, with interruptions
~d.scr(1);         // pure chance every slice
~d.ord;            // ord and rev also reset scr to 0
::

SUBSECTION:: Euclidean gating

code::
~d.mask(5, 16);    // keep 5 of 16 slices, rest the others
~d.mask(3, 8);
~d.ord;            // undo
::

SUBSECTION:: Rolls

code::
~d.roll(2);        // replay the first half of each slice, twice
~d.roll(4);        // stutter
~d.roll(1);        // off
::

SECTION:: Pitch: fitted or preserved

There are two honest ways to make a loop recorded at one tempo play at another,
and code::vari:: is the fader between them.

code::
~d.vari(1);        // default: repitch the break to fit — the sampler / jungle sound
~d.vari(0);        // keep the original pitch and let the grains do the stretching
::

At code::vari(1):: the whole loop is resampled to fit the bar, so it stays
internally coherent at any code::grind:: — including code::grind(0)::, where you
hear the plain repitched loop. This is what almost every hardware sampler did, and
what jungle is made of.

At code::vari(0):: grains play at the original speed and the emphasis::pointer::
does the fitting. That needs grains to work: with only a few large ones, each grain
overruns its own slice and drags the next slice's audio in behind it. So pitch
preservation wants code::grind:: above about 0.4:

code::
~d.vari(0); ~d.grind(0.7);    // original pitch, properly stretched
~d.speed(1.5);                // and transpose on purpose, on top of either mode
~d.flip(0.3);                 // a third of grains play backwards
::

note::
code::speed:: is called that, and not code::rate::, because
link::Classes/NodeProxy:: already has a code::rate:: method (it reports audio vs
control). Shadowing it would break JITLib.
::

SECTION:: Swapping the sample

code::
~d.brk(\think);    // a different recording, on the next bar, no gap
~d.brk;            // no argument: cycle to the next break in the library
::

Nothing else changes. Your grind, your sequence, your overlap, your window all
stay exactly where you left them, because they live on the proxy, not on the
sample. The swap is gapless for a mechanical reason worth knowing: the engine's
grain generator reads which buffer to use once per grain, so grains already
sounding finish on the old break while new grains start reading the new one. It
crossfades itself.

If the new loop has a different beat length, the loop boundary moves — so the
swap also realigns the grid to the bar. You can ask for that alignment at any time:

code::
~d.resync;
::

SECTION:: When the tempo changes

code::
150.bpm;           // or p.clock.tempo = 2.5
::

Every live break voice follows immediately. Tempo is not baked into anything: the
engine takes it as a control, the registry pushes the clock's value to every
voice, and a watcher catches tempo changes made any other way.

SECTION:: Chopping with patterns instead

Everything above is one long-running voice — a canvas you sculpt. Sometimes you
want the other thing: one synth per chop, so that per-hit pitch, panning,
amplitude and effects are ordinary pattern work. That is code::chop::, and it
returns a plain link::Classes/Pbind:::

code::
~c = \demo.chop("0325");
~c.play;

// it is a real Pbind, so all the usual composition applies
~c = \demo.chop("0.32 0123", amp: 0.4);
~c = \demo.chop(nil, steps: 8);              // the whole loop in eighths
~c = \demo.chop("0123").fast(2);             // Synesthesia transform verbs
~c = Pwrand([\demo.chop("0123"), \demo.chop("0321")], [0.8, 0.2], inf);
::

Use the canvas voice (code::brk::) when you want to sculpt texture and dissolve
into grains. Use the brush (code::chop::) when you want per-event control and
pattern logic. They are happy at the same time on the same break.

SECTION:: Telling it the truth about your loops

A drum loop file does not say what tempo it was recorded at, so the registry
guesses, in this order:

numberedlist::
## a bpm-tagged number in the filename — code::amen_175bpm.wav::, code::loop-175-BPM.wav::, code::bpm140.wav::;
## a bpm-tagged or plausible number in the folder name — code::breaks175/::;
## the duration, assuming the file is cut to a whole number of beats, preferring
   the reading whose tempo is most break-plausible. A bare number in the filename
   is used to break ties.
::

Check what it decided:

code::
Break.list;
::

The code::from:: column tells you which rule fired, so a guess is never invisible.
When it guesses wrong, correct it once and it is remembered:

code::
\demo.bpm_(172);       // this loop is 172 bpm
\demo.len_(8);         // this loop is 8 beats long
::

Either call writes a code::_breaks.scd:: sidecar next to the audio, which is
merged over the inference at every boot. It is a plain, hand-editable file — and
because it can also rename a break, it is how an unwieldy download becomes a short
name you can type mid-set:

code::
Dictionary[
	"250093__amfe__175bpm-amen-punchy-loop.wav" -> (key: 'amen', bpm: 175, beats: 4)
]
::

warning::
code::len_:: sets the length in beats, and beats is the number the engine actually
uses — a wrong beat count is what makes a loop play at half or double speed. If a
break sounds like it is running at the wrong tempo, that is the first thing to
fix, not code::speed::.
::

SECTION:: Chopping your own playing

A recording made at the session tempo needs no inference at all, so a live capture
is the most reliable break in the library:

code::
r = rec(8);                        // capture 8 beats of input
Break.capture(\me, r, 8);          // register it, exactly
~e.brk(\me); ~e.play;
~e.grind(0.6); ~e.scr(0.4);
::

SECTION:: A short set

code::
p.clock.tempo = 2.9;                        // 174 bpm
~d.brk(\amen); ~d.play;                     // in on the bar, locked
~d.grind(0.5); ~d.win(\punch);              // clean chops
~d.seq("0.325476");                         // a phrase, with a rest
~d.scr(0.35);                               // let chance in a little
~d.brk(\think);                             // new source, same shape
~d.roll(4);                                 // stutter
~d.roll(1); ~d.grind(0.95); ~d.jit(0.6);    // dissolve
~d.over(3); ~d.route(\verb, 0.5);           // and it is a pad now
~d.ord; ~d.grind(0.4); ~d.over(1);          // reassemble
~c = \amen.chop("0246").fast(2); ~c.play;   // a pattern layer over it
hush(8);
::

SECTION:: What to expect, honestly

list::
## Tempo inference assumes loops are cut to whole beats. Ragged cuts will guess
   wrong; code::Break.list:: shows you which ones were guesses, and one
   code::len_:: fixes each permanently.
## The middle of the code::grind:: range — where chops become grains — is
   mathematically continuous, but whether every part of it is emphasis::musically::
   useful is something only your ears settle. If some region turns out to be dead,
   say so; the fix is a curve change, not an architecture change.
## Grid alignment is performance-grade, not sample-locked-forever. It is exact when
   a voice starts and after any code::resync::; a mid-set tempo change can leave
   the loop boundary off the bar until you resync.
::

SECTION:: See also

link::Classes/Break:: for the registry API, link::Reference/Synesthesia-Reference::
for the whole vocabulary on one page, and code::Setup/BreakTour.scd:: in the repo
for a scripted walk through the regions described above.
